this is a production hi there my name is  zachary stancil i am the hemp germplasm  curator at the plant genetic resources  unit  which is a part of the national plant  germplasm system which is in turn part  of the agricultural research service  which is part of the usda  my background i'm a recent graduate from  cornell horticulture where i primarily  focused on brassica oloracia crops  specifically broccoli  my work was done in support of the  eastern broccoli project which is a  10-year study um  breeding developing  broccoli hybrids adapted for  i'm really excited to tell you about the  conditions  so we are in a  one of the plant genetic resources unit  greenhouse  in beautiful geneva new york where we  maintain  a wide variety of different vegetable  crop collections so we  have about 20 000  accessions that are comprised of about  300 unique species  just like the other vegetable crop  collections held at pgru  we don't actively participate in plant  breeding rather we  function to collect conserve  characterize and distribute these  materials so that's what we'll do with  hemp as well  so  we're acquiring materials from uh  many different sources a lot of our  initial donors are going to be  university level or private breeding  companies that are working with hemp  germplasm um we the regulatory  definition exclusively  is given in the 2020 hemp final rule  which is defined essentially as  less than 0.3 percent thc by dry weight  um so so those are the materials that we  will uh collect  increase characterize and distribute um  so  much of the work that we do here will  follow a very similar pipeline to how we  would for example deal with the allium  crops brassica crops tomato collections  as well so we maintain these germplasm  collections for a couple reasons one to  simply just conserve  the uh crop genetic diversity of these  many different crops  and then secondly we're a working germ  plasma repository so we make these  materials available  to  many 